  "notes": "Penn State Erie, The Behrend College. No University Park equivalent exists (UP has no generic, individualized 'Science, B.S.' major -- its science students declare a named science major instead), so this is built fresh -- not a Pattern A/B disambiguation case. Sourced from Behrend's own bulletin page (bulletins.psu.edu/undergraduate/colleges/behrend/science-bs/), Plan Code SCNBC_BS, minimum 120 credits, at least 15 credits at the 400-level. Four named options exist (General Science, Environmental Studies, Earth and Space Science Pre-Certification, General Science Pre-Certification -- the latter two are teacher-certification tracks requiring ASTRO/GEOSC/METEO coursework); this plan models the General Science Option specifically, an individualized-science degree built around a single 18-credit concentration area (computer sciences, life sciences, mathematical sciences, or physical sciences -- left as generic 'Science Supporting List'/'Program List' slots since the bulletin names the category, not specific courses a student in this option would pick, and which one of the four areas a given student picks is exactly the kind of individual choice this major is built around) plus a wider unenumerated department-list pool. Modeled as plain Supporting-Course-style placeholder slots (this repo's existing convention for an unenumerated department list), rather than open_elective slots, because the bulletin's categories name specific *areas* (pick one of four departments) rather than a single excludes-based denylist open_elective is built to model. CMPSC 121's real prereq is MATH 110 (Business Calculus), mutually exclusive for credit with the MATH 140 sequence this plan (like every science student here) actually takes -- modeled as a 3-option list (CMPSC 121/200/201), same convention and same reasoning as PHYSBH-2026.json. World language (8cr total) modeled as two generic 4-credit slots since the bulletin names no specific language. Physics Sequence A (PHYS 211/212/213) is modeled; Sequence B (PHYS 250/251, no third course) was not, matching the bulletin's own suggested plan for this option. Post-prereq-chain fix (same real issue documented across every CHEM 110-requiring plan in this repo): CHEM 110's real prereq is MATH 22, which chains through MATH 21, MATH 4, and MATH 3 -- added explicitly (Semester 1: MATH 3/4; Semester 2: MATH 21/22); max_credits_per_semester set to 18 for the same reason as every other such plan.",
